Yes. I tightened the standard substantially after checking the earlier 100. The previous census was useful for discovery, but its own `U?` category explicitly meant “probably untranslated; verify before claiming first translation,” so those cannot be treated as confirmed. 

More importantly, I found several **false positives** while doing the audit. For example, Kāraṇāgama Kriyāpāda, Makuṭāgama Kriyā/Caryā, Candrajñānāgama Kriyā/Caryā and Pārameśvarāgama already have published English translations. ([Vedic Reserve][1]) So I am **not going to manufacture “100 confirmed untranslated” by padding the list**.

The defensible claim I can test is:

> **No complete scholarly English translation located as of 13 August 2026**, while a Sanskrit witness/edition is demonstrably extant.

Absolute proof that nobody anywhere has ever translated something is impossible. But bibliographic audit + exact-title searches + specialist surveys can get us very close.

